"""
Real-time pulse extraction using the POS algorithm.

This is a real-time streaming adaptation of the original offline
pulse.py script. Per explicit request:

    * pos_algorithm(), bandpass_filter(), estimate_heart_rate(), and all
      of the MediaPipe-based face/skin ROI extraction (landmarks_to_pixels,
      build_roi_mask, mean_skin_rgb, build_face_landmarker,
      ensure_face_landmarker_model) are copied VERBATIM from the original
      script. Nothing in the algorithm, filtering, or post-processing was
      touched.
    * The only thing that changed is the video-input / orchestration
      layer: instead of reading a fixed [start, end) frame range from a
      file and running the algorithm once at the end, this script:
        1. streams frames continuously from a camera (or a file, paced
           to real time) on the main thread, never blocking on HR
           estimation,
        2. builds a mean-RGB trace incrementally, one row per frame,
        3. does NOT display any heart-rate value for the first 10
           seconds (t = 0..11s exclusive) while it collects the initial
           buffer,
        4. at t = 11s, runs pos_algorithm/bandpass_filter/
           estimate_heart_rate on the window covering samples 0..10s and
           displays the result,
        5. at t = 12s, runs the same algorithm on the window 0..11s and
           updates the displayed value, at t = 13s on 0..12s, and so on
           -- i.e. the window grows by one second every second, exactly
           as specified, while acquisition keeps running uninterrupted
           in the background.

NOTE ON WINDOW SHAPE: the spec given ("0-10s", then "0-11s", then
"0-12s"...) describes a window that starts at 0 and grows forever, not a
fixed-width 10s buffer that slides forward (e.g. 1-11s, 2-12s, ...).
This script implements the growing window exactly as specified. If a
true fixed-width rolling 10s window is actually what's wanted, only
`_process_window()`'s slicing needs to change (see the comment there).

Requires: pip install mediapipe opencv-python scipy numpy
"""

